<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I Can Count! – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Hold Up Ten Fingers!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Can Count!</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: productive: Write simple words, with model guidance. interactive: Engage in interactive labeling activities.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write simple words with teacher support (e.g., 'desk' or 'book'). Can engage in interactive labeling activities by identifying and labeling objects in shared drawings (e.g., writing labels under numbers supported with pictures representing those numbers). Can practice writing numbers, with model guidance (e.g., writing the numbers from one to ten following a number chart with labels).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; text-align: left;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; text-align: left;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; text-align: left;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads students in a "Ten Little Fingers" song with actions (TPR), counting fingers and showing them. "One little, two little, three little fingers..." up to ten. The teacher then asks students to "show me five fingers!" and "show me ten fingers!" to review counting and finger recognition. The teacher writes large numbers 1-5 on the board and asks students to "write the number in the air" with their finger.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard with the numeral '3' and a picture of three pencils. "Look! This is number three. T-H-R-E-E. Three pencils." The teacher writes the word "three" clearly on the board, pointing to each letter as it is written. Then, the teacher holds up a large picture of a hand and points to a finger, saying, "This is a finger. F-I-N-G-E-R. Finger." and writes "finger" next to it, emphasizing the initial /f/ sound. The teacher models tracing the word "finger" with a finger on a desk.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to the number 3 and the word "three" on the board) "Is this number five?" (No) "What number is this?" (Three!)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to the word "finger") "Is this a hand or a finger?" (A finger!) "Can you show me your finger?" (Students show finger)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ / _______</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large flashcards showing numerals (1-10) with corresponding quantities of common classroom objects (e.g., '1 book', '2 pencils', '3 crayons', '4 blocks', '5 fingers'). A number chart (1-10) showing both numeral and word form. A large picture of a child's hand for labeling.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The learner comprehends and produces short, simple, concrete, and routine messages about daily classroom objects and counting, focusing on writing simple words and numbers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activities & Teacher Actions:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher displays the number chart (1-10 with words). Teacher points to each number, counts aloud, and says the word. "One... O-N-E." Emphasize initial /k/ in 'count' and /f/ in 'fingers' when using related words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows flashcards one by one, asking "What number is this?" and "How many books/pencils/blocks?". Students respond orally.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to the word 'hand' written clearly on the chart and asks students to trace it in the air. "Let's write 'hand' in the air."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Comprehension tasks: Students point to the correct flashcard when the teacher calls out a number (e.g., "Point to 'four blocks'!"). Students hold up the correct number of fingers.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ / _______</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Writing)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice writing numbers (1-10) and simple related nouns (e.g., 'hand', 'finger', 'book', 'pencil', 'crayon') with model guidance. They will use grammar structures like 'Quantity + nouns' (e.g., 'two books').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Tracing Numbers and Words (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher distributes worksheets with dotted outlines of numerals 1-10 and the corresponding number words (one-ten). Students trace the numerals and number words using pencils or crayons. The teacher circulates, providing individual support and checking letter formation. Teacher encourages saying the number/word aloud while tracing.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Picture Labeling with Word Bank (Semi-controlled):</b> Students receive a worksheet with various pictures of classroom objects in quantities from 1 to 5 (e.g., 2 blocks, 1 book, 3 pencils, 4 crayons). Below each picture, there is a blank line. At the top of the sheet, there's a word bank with words like 'block', 'book', 'pencil', 'crayon', and numbers 'one', 'two', 'three', 'four'. Students identify the object and quantity, then copy the correct word and numeral from the word bank onto the line. E.g., for "3 pencils," they write "3" and "pencils."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Interactive Hand Labeling (Peer Check):</b> In pairs, students receive a worksheet with an outline of a hand. One student traces their own hand on the paper. Then, using a model word card 'finger', they help each other to write 'finger' next to one of the fingers on the drawing. The teacher provides model numbers (1-5) and guides students to write numbers 1-5 on the fingers. Students can then show their labeled hand to another pair and say, "One finger, two fingers..." to practice pronunciation of initial /f/.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ / _______</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-17 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Fluency focus on Writing)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual written product and collaborative labeling.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activities & Teacher Actions:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>"My Counting Book" Creation:</b> The teacher gives each student a small blank booklet (stapled pages). On each page, there is a space for a drawing and a line below it. Students choose a number from 1 to 5. They draw that quantity of an object (e.g., 'three blocks' or 'two books') on one page. Then, they write the numeral and the corresponding word for the number and the object below the drawing. The teacher walks around, assisting with spelling and number formation using the number chart and vocabulary word cards as models. E.g., for three red blocks, they write '3' and 'three red blocks'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Labeling the Classroom Drawing:</b> The teacher projects a large drawing of a simple classroom scene on the board (or distributes a printed version). The drawing contains visible objects like 5 pencils, 1 book, 2 crayons, 1 teacher's desk. The teacher points to an object, "What is this? How many pencils?" Students call out answers. Then, the teacher models writing the number and the word (e.g., '5 pencils') next to the objects. Students then copy the labels onto their own drawing, identifying and writing the numbers and simple words for at least 3-4 objects. This promotes interactive labeling.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students present their "My Counting Book" to a partner, showing their drawings and pointing to the words they wrote. Sociolinguistic: Encouraging turn-taking and active listening as they share their work.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ / _______</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative Assessment)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Observation Checklist. The teacher observes students' work during the "My Counting Book" activity and the classroom labeling. A checklist is used to note if students can: a) write numerals 1-5, b) write simple number words (e.g., 'one', 'two', 'three'), and c) copy simple noun labels (e.g., 'book', 'pencil') with model guidance.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their "My Counting Book" and their labeled classroom drawing. The teacher checks the legibility of numbers and words, and the accuracy of labeling based on the models provided.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Legibility:</b> Can the teacher read the numbers and words written by the student?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Does the student attempt to write target number words (one-five) and noun labels (book, pencil, block, crayon)?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Following Model:</b> Does the student demonstrate the ability to copy words and numbers from a model?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> The teacher asks students:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I write my numbers nicely?" (Thumbs up/down)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I write 'pencil'?" (Thumbs up/down)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ / _______</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What word was easy to write today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What number did you like to write?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well in this writing lesson for Kinder? The tracing and guided copying activities provided good scaffolding. Students enjoyed drawing and labeling their own 'Counting Books'. What adjustments are needed for the next lesson? Some students struggled with letter formation for longer words like 'crayon' even with models, so perhaps breaking down words into sounds and using more tactile writing (sand trays) could be beneficial. Need to reinforce the initial /k/ and /f/ sounds more explicitly during writing practice. Scaffolding for writing numbers 6-10 will be important in future lessons.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This writing practice will prepare students for Lesson 5, where they will consolidate their learning by labeling and writing about objects as part of a larger project, focusing on identifying quantities and names of objects up to ten, and integrating all four skills.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ / _______</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>